---
import { FORMATS } from '../../data/formats';
---

<section class="fo-band" id="formats">
  <div class="fo-band-head">
    <h2>What it reads</h2>
    <p>
      Type is decided by magic bytes first, extension second, and the server's
      <code>Content-Type</code> last — so a PNG named <code>.txt</code> still renders as an image.
      Anything unrecognised gets a download card, never a broken view.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="fo-formats">
    {
      FORMATS.map((row) => (
        <article class="fo-fmt" data-cat={row.cat}>
          <div class="fo-fmt-top">
            <span class="fo-fmt-name">{row.name}</span>
            <span class="fo-fmt-fidelity">{row.fidelity}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fo-fmt-exts">
            {row.exts.map((ext) => (
              <span class="fo-ext">{ext}</span>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="fo-fmt-engine">{row.engine}</p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="fo-footnote">
    Fidelity is stated plainly: <em>exact</em> means pixel-accurate, <em>text only</em> means the
    words and tables survive but the formatting does not. For Office files at a public URL, the
    toolbar can hand off to the Microsoft viewer for a full-fidelity render.
  </p>
</section>
